in a way that is just superficial.

00:31:56.480 --> 00:32:00.720
If one person is getting abused in the city of Atlanta

00:32:01.200 --> 00:32:04.280
as a Muslim, who else would stand up for them?

00:32:04.960 --> 00:32:06.700
People don't have food to eat.

00:32:07.039 --> 00:32:13.099
People are struggling with mental health conditions, wellness conditions, financial anxiety,

00:32:13.760 --> 00:32:17.120
deportation struggles, all kinds of things.

00:32:18.960 --> 00:32:22.379
How is it not something that is my responsibility?

00:32:22.799 --> 00:32:27.820
And when tawa comes in to the picture, consciousness awakens,

00:32:28.559 --> 00:32:29.919
I'm conscious of what?

00:32:29.919 --> 00:32:31.499
The divine. And why?

00:32:31.679 --> 00:32:33.739
Because I'm going to stand in front of him one day.

00:32:34.640 --> 00:32:36.840
And what did I turn this dean into?

00:32:37.679 --> 00:32:41.840
The last thing I'll say, and I'm sorry that I talk a lot, is that you

00:32:41.840 --> 00:32:43.840
got to actually find joy in it.

00:32:45.039 --> 00:32:46.420
You should want to

00:32:46.880 --> 00:32:48.600
be in a place where

00:32:49.039 --> 00:32:49.439
you

00:32:50.080 --> 00:32:52.520
help people in their time of need.

00:32:53.200 --> 00:32:59.360
To get excited about the idea that my dean teaches me that I should be the

00:32:59.360 --> 00:33:03.820
one that's assisting people through their pain and through their struggle.

00:33:04.320 --> 00:33:10.799
My dean is rooted in the legacy of a man sallallahu alaihi wasallam who met people

00:33:10.799 --> 00:33:16.880
where they were did not find them to be people that he broke down but he

00:33:16.880 --> 00:33:17.619
built up

00:33:18.240 --> 00:33:23.120
and helped them to recognize what was inherently good with them and didn't leave them to

00:33:23.120 --> 00:33:25.180
go through their struggles on their own.

00:33:26.000 --> 00:33:28.480
How are you going to know what somebody goes through if you don't talk to them

00:33:28.480 --> 00:33:29.480
and ask them?

00:33:30.159 --> 00:33:34.000
When was the last time you went to a masid and the leadership of the masid

00:33:34.000 --> 00:33:36.880
asked you, "What do you think we should be doing?"

00:33:37.440 --> 00:33:39.660
rather than just imposing upon you.

00:33:39.679 --> 00:33:46.940
How can any one person fundamentally know what thousands of people with diverse complexity

00:33:47.360 --> 00:33:50.679
to their lived experience and identity just need?

00:33:50.960 --> 00:33:52.379
This is what this woman did.

00:33:52.559 --> 00:33:55.139
She said to me, "I know what everybody needs.

00:33:55.840 --> 00:33:57.100
I'm not judging her,

00:33:57.600 --> 00:34:03.440
but how is that rooted in the sunnah, in the way that you see and think

00:34:03.440 --> 00:34:04.460
and perceive

00:34:05.440 --> 00:34:10.900
versus who is the one that thought they knew everything and better than anyone else?"

00:34:11.359 --> 00:34:13.099
That's not our tradition.

00:34:13.359 --> 00:34:18.720
That's the sunnah of a tradition that we want to stay away from and seek refuge

00:34:18.720 --> 00:34:23.040
from. I yield to the fact that I don't know and then I go find out

00:34:23.040 --> 00:34:28.000
what I don't know so then I'm doing based off of actual knowledge and not just

00:34:28.000 --> 00:34:29.740
my whims and my conjecture.

00:34:29.839 --> 00:34:30.879
Do you see what I mean?

00:34:30.879 --> 00:34:31.339
Mhm.

00:34:31.919 --> 00:34:32.259
So

00:34:32.720 --> 00:34:38.079
leading to that, what practical advice would you give us whether it's the Muslim wellness center

00:34:38.079 --> 00:34:42.079
or a few of the other administrations or the messages that actually are here?

00:34:42.079 --> 00:34:45.180
They want to actually build conscious communities and also

00:34:45.679 --> 00:34:50.800
they want to be able to listen and hear what the community says and have an

00:34:50.800 --> 00:34:56.000
open ear. What practical advice did you think has worked in your experience or that you

00:34:56.000 --> 00:35:00.240
have seen that here here are the three things you guys should start or you know

00:35:00.240 --> 00:35:03.660
that could really put you on the path because it's it's it's it's

00:35:04.079 --> 00:35:06.240
pro it's a progressive journey.

00:35:06.240 --> 00:35:07.520
We're going to fumble.

00:35:07.520 --> 00:35:08.800
We're going to learn fumble.

00:35:08.800 --> 00:35:12.860
But just making sure that our trajectory is in the right direction.

00:35:13.119 --> 00:35:17.320
Well, if you answer questions for yourself, what does success look like?

00:35:17.680 --> 00:35:19.420
Anybody could build something.

00:35:19.520 --> 00:35:20.599
Do you know what I mean?

00:35:21.520 --> 00:35:23.980
The idea is not to just build a building

00:35:24.560 --> 00:35:29.999
because there's a lot of masids in this country that nobody prays in anymore

00:35:30.480 --> 00:35:32.340
because they didn't build withan

00:35:33.920 --> 00:35:36.460
objective fact. It's not judgmental.

00:35:37.280 --> 00:35:38.539
And then they conjecture

00:35:39.280 --> 00:35:40.840
without real data.

00:35:41.680 --> 00:35:43.680
This is why young people don't come.

00:35:43.680 --> 00:35:44.940
Here's the problem.

00:35:44.960 --> 00:35:46.640
But what are you basing it off of?

00:35:46.720 --> 00:35:47.720
How do you know?

00:35:48.400 --> 00:35:53.280
And when you go in with an assumption, everything that you process is rooted through the

00:35:53.280 --> 00:35:56.140
prism of that assumption and proving the assumption

00:35:56.960 --> 00:36:03.200
versus understanding what our tradition is rooted in and finding a sense of empowerment through it.

00:36:03.280 --> 00:36:05.500
Not a sense of anxiety, humiliation.

00:36:05.680 --> 00:36:09.759
Why does Imam Malik say I don't know to 36 questions out of 40?

00:36:10.400 --> 00:36:10.860
Why?

00:36:12.640 --> 00:36:17.420
And what does it then enable as a next step in relation to that process?

00:36:17.680 --> 00:36:18.960
It's okay to say

00:36:19.359 --> 00:36:24.640
there's this hole I don't know how to fill it rather than pretending like I do.

00:36:24.720 --> 00:36:28.880
And when you say I don't know, then you can engage the people that are going

00:36:28.880 --> 00:36:30.220
to be able to help you.

00:36:30.800 --> 00:36:36.320
You want to have community engagement that has multiple entry points to it.

00:36:36.400 --> 00:36:38.220
Look in this room right now.

00:36:38.320 --> 00:36:40.520
There's like 50 of us in the room.

00:36:40.640 --> 00:36:45.900
And how different are all of you racially, culturally, in terms of your age?

00:36:46.240 --> 00:36:50.160
I don't know what anyone's socioeconomic reality is.

00:36:50.480 --> 00:36:52.780
I don't know like a lot of you.

00:36:52.800 --> 00:36:56.720
So I can't tell you what your lives have been like, but in every which way

00:36:56.720 --> 00:36:57.780
you can assume

00:36:58.160 --> 00:37:02.200
people have diverse experience in the room.

00:37:03.760 --> 00:37:09.340
So one of the best ways to be able to understand the needs of particular demographics

00:37:09.599 --> 00:37:10.920
is to ask them.

00:37:11.280 --> 00:37:16.299
In our community right now, we get about five people a week who take their shahada.

00:37:16.960 --> 00:37:20.539
And we're not standing on a street corner passing out pamphlets.

00:37:20.560 --> 00:37:22.520
I'm not knocking anybody who does.

00:37:22.560 --> 00:37:23.900
They just make their way.

00:37:24.160 --> 00:37:29.040
Our community, amongst the many things that it's known for, it's known as a place where

00:37:29.040 --> 00:37:31.420
people come to take their shahada.

00:37:31.920 --> 00:37:34.200
And I find joy in that.

00:37:35.359 --> 00:37:38.940
Understanding and recognizing when we built out a

00:37:39.599 --> 00:37:42.140
program for converts in our community.

00:37:42.800 --> 00:37:44.160
My wife is a convert.

00:37:44.160 --> 00:37:49.040
She took her shahada maybe 27 years ago, well before I met her, has practiced Islam

00:37:49.040 --> 00:37:50.280
longer than I have.

00:37:50.480 --> 00:37:54.580
I've sat down with thousands of people who have taken their shahada at this point.

00:37:54.640 --> 00:38:00.140
And despite having a deep intimate relationship with someone who's a convert and sitting with many

00:38:00.160 --> 00:38:04.800
people who have gone through the conversion process, I still can't tell you what it's like

00:38:04.800 --> 00:38:07.280
to convert. I was born into a Muslim family.

00:38:07.599 --> 00:38:10.740
Who's going to tell me what it's like to convert to Islam?

00:38:11.040 --> 00:38:12.239
Somebody who's done it.

00:38:13.119 --> 00:38:18.100
So 15 years ago when we started a convert program at our center

00:38:18.800 --> 00:38:19.100
I

00:38:19.520 --> 00:38:22.520
had a much smaller community base at that time.

00:38:22.560 --> 00:38:25.680
Our community now we estimate is about 20,000 people.

00:38:25.680 --> 00:38:27.520
Alhamdulillah. And it grows.

00:38:27.520 --> 00:38:29.960
It started from a very small base.

00:38:30.320 --> 00:38:31.180
In 1978

00:38:32.000 --> 00:38:35.440
five students found it and it's grown now to impact.

00:38:35.440 --> 00:38:38.539
And I'll come back to this a little bit later if I remember.

00:38:38.720 --> 00:38:41.819
But when we did this more than a decade ago,

00:38:42.240 --> 00:38:45.819
I sat down with a hundred converts in our community

00:38:46.240 --> 00:38:50.179
one- on-one and I just asked them to tell me their story.

00:38:50.560 --> 00:38:52.320
What was it like to convert?

00:38:52.320 --> 00:38:53.740
What was helpful to you?

00:38:53.839 --> 00:38:55.020
What was difficult?

00:38:55.119 --> 00:38:58.220
How was it engaging Muslims after you converted?

00:38:58.320 --> 00:39:00.620
What kind of resources do you wish you had?

00:39:00.720 --> 00:39:02.800
What kind of advice would you give to people?

00:39:02.800 --> 00:39:05.900
The questions then yielded more responses.

00:39:06.160 --> 00:39:10.320
And because they have a relationship with me, there's a trust that's there.

00:39:10.320 --> 00:39:13.220
May Allah make me one who honors that trust.

00:39:13.440 --> 00:39:17.100
They're willing to speak and make themselves vulnerable.

00:39:17.359 --> 00:39:21.400
Conversations lasted for two to three hours at a time.

00:39:22.000 --> 00:39:25.220
And I was just sitting and listening and taking notes.

00:39:25.680 --> 00:39:27.819
And then we took those narratives

00:39:28.400 --> 00:39:31.040
and analyzed it for trends and themes.

00:39:31.599 --> 00:39:34.880
And then from there we built out a program

00:39:36.240 --> 00:39:37.840
hearing from the people.

00:39:38.160 --> 00:39:44.000
At our center we have a women's initiative, a converts group, a Latino Muslim group, a

00:39:44.000 --> 00:39:47.140
black Muslim initiative, a professionals group.

00:39:47.280 --> 00:39:53.599
We laid the foundation for other entry points as well in addition to at New York

00:39:53.599 --> 00:39:59.119
University which was our initial base of operation where there were 12 MSAs not in competition

00:39:59.119 --> 00:40:01.840
with each other but just doing different things.

00:40:03.599 --> 00:40:08.460
There's 4,000 Muslim students as well as students that came from all over the city

00:40:09.040 --> 00:40:13.140
from other colleges that didn't have similar accommodations in addition to

00:40:13.520 --> 00:40:18.240
many people who live and work in the area that just see our space as their

00:40:18.240 --> 00:40:18.940
community.

00:40:19.599 --> 00:40:23.180
So, we were going to lay the foundation for an artist collective.

00:40:23.280 --> 00:40:25.740
We had a families group that we were building out.

00:40:25.839 --> 00:40:28.240
When COVID got in the middle, we derailed it.

00:40:28.240 --> 00:40:33.599
But out of our center, we've also built a domestic violence agency and operate a shelter

00:40:33.599 --> 00:40:36.960
in the Bronx for women and children who are survivors of abuse.

00:40:36.960 --> 00:40:42.380
We have a charitable fund that supports 800 families in need in our community.

00:40:43.040 --> 00:40:45.440
We have a weekend learning program for children.

00:40:45.440 --> 00:40:48.140
We're incubating a full-time school right now.

00:40:48.240 --> 00:40:51.440
I'm just trying to build as much as I can while Allah has given me breaths

00:40:51.440 --> 00:40:55.220
to breathe in this world and building with people who want to build.

00:40:55.680 --> 00:40:57.800
It's rooted in data.

00:40:59.839 --> 00:41:06.319
And when I've shared with people different things or hosted conversations with people like panels of

00:41:06.319 --> 00:41:12.400
converts with 10, 20 converts, giving them an opportunity to not just share the story of

00:41:12.400 --> 00:41:16.960
their conversion, which is where most of us start and stop, but then I say, "What

00:41:16.960 --> 00:41:20.079
is it like after you converted and after you became Muslim?"

00:41:20.079 --> 00:41:22.740
and they talk about just how terrible it is

00:41:23.119 --> 00:41:26.299
dealing with Muslims in Muslim communities.

00:41:27.359 --> 00:41:31.359
And then I have people who are humble enough in the audience to come up to

00:41:31.359 --> 00:41:37.720
me and they said, "I've ran the convert care program, the da program in my masid

00:41:37.920 --> 00:41:43.119
for 10, 15 years and I just realized I don't know a thing about what I'm

00:41:43.119 --> 00:41:44.340
doing." Ya Allah.

00:41:44.800 --> 00:41:46.760
And that's the kind of person we need.

00:41:47.200 --> 00:41:48.400
one who can say

00:41:48.800 --> 00:41:51.500
I'm not doing it the way it should be done

00:41:51.920 --> 00:41:53.500
not somebody who pretends

00:41:54.000 --> 00:41:59.440
and then there's data to it not I think I feel I want how many of

00:41:59.440 --> 00:42:03.500
you have ever woken up in the morning and thought about my grandfather

00:42:04.160 --> 00:42:08.800
none of you it doesn't make you a bad person why would you think about people

00:42:08.800 --> 00:42:12.800
that you don't think about who you have ever interacted with you didn't wake up in

00:42:12.800 --> 00:42:16.800
the morning and say you know I wonder how Khal Latif's grandparents are

00:42:18.240 --> 00:42:22.160
In the same vein, you're not going to think about the people who don't enter your

00:42:22.160 --> 00:42:22.860
consciousness.

00:42:23.680 --> 00:42:26.680
Doesn't mean you don't have responsibility towards them.

00:42:27.440 --> 00:42:30.400
Doesn't mean that they still don't have rights over us.

00:42:31.440 --> 00:42:35.839
and understanding the best person to tell you what their needs are are going to be

00:42:35.839 --> 00:42:36.659
the person

00:42:37.119 --> 00:42:42.960
beyond the individual interaction that necessitates building a trust with somebody that they're willing to make

00:42:42.960 --> 00:42:45.420
themselves vulnerable with confidentiality

00:42:46.079 --> 00:42:48.479
and say this is what this is like for me.

00:42:50.000 --> 00:42:55.040
You get to a place where you can do broader needs assessment and you instill within

00:42:55.040 --> 00:42:56.539
your community development

00:42:56.960 --> 00:43:03.760
people who create spaces that are both individualized as well as communal that allow for people

00:43:03.760 --> 00:43:09.260
to share so that you can understand a little bit about somebody who's different from you.

00:43:09.599 --> 00:43:12.500
I got thousands of people's stories in my heart.

00:43:12.720 --> 00:43:15.080
When people ask me how do the words flow,

00:43:15.440 --> 00:43:19.780
I got so many people who have trusted me with what they carry.

00:43:20.160 --> 00:43:22.059
It's going to inform perspective

00:43:22.480 --> 00:43:28.640
that is rooted in what is authentically our tradition, not rooted just that my life is

00:43:28.640 --> 00:43:30.659
the only way life has been lived.

00:43:31.280 --> 00:43:33.819
And this is why you got to think it's objective.

00:43:33.839 --> 00:43:34.880
Just think about it.

00:43:35.040 --> 00:43:40.880
If you have a masid, you have a nonprofit, you have a for-profit, you know why

00:43:40.880 --> 00:43:44.000
a lot of us go through what we go through in this country?

00:43:44.000 --> 00:43:50.560
Because pretty much every entity and institution is still run by people who check off every

00:43:50.560 --> 00:43:51.819
box of privilege.

00:43:51.839 --> 00:43:54.140
The white Protestant male

00:43:54.560 --> 00:44:02.800
runs every single thing from city electeds to state electives to national electeds to coaches in

00:44:02.800 --> 00:44:06.460
various professional sports leagues to heads of universities,

00:44:06.880 --> 00:44:08.539
CEOs of companies.

00:44:08.640 --> 00:44:12.700
This is why you and I have to struggle to even let our kids miss

00:44:13.119 --> 00:44:18.800
because the people at the decision table, they're making decisions about us without any frame of

00:44:18.800 --> 00:44:19.500
consciousness.

00:44:20.000 --> 00:44:21.840
It's not enjoyable, is it?

00:44:23.280 --> 00:44:28.240
So, what happens when everybody who runs your masid is the exact same version of the

00:44:28.240 --> 00:44:29.780
other people that run the masid?

00:44:30.640 --> 00:44:34.460
If every single person is a South Asian man like you,

00:44:34.880 --> 00:44:41.260
if every single person comes from the same cultural, ethnic, lived experience as you,

00:44:41.760 --> 00:44:44.059
you ain't wake up thinking about my grandfather.

00:44:44.880 --> 00:44:48.400
When was the last time you woke up thinking about, hey, how do we meet the

00:44:48.400 --> 00:44:51.819
needs of people who are dealing with these lived realities?

00:44:52.640 --> 00:44:55.900
And if the only presence at the table is stereotypical,

00:44:56.480 --> 00:45:00.760
well then you see the consequence in anti-black society of how that trickles down.

00:45:00.800 --> 00:45:02.880
Don't make those people your teacher.

00:45:02.880 --> 00:45:06.059
Your teacher sallallahu alaihi wasallam built differently.

00:45:06.319 --> 00:45:09.680
He built a community before he built an ummah.

00:45:09.680 --> 00:45:17.520
And that community was built on shared internals, cultivating iman in something that then allowed for

00:45:17.520 --> 00:45:23.680
there to be a growth trajectory that started with handfuls and his last is given to

00:45:23.680 --> 00:45:25.780
over 120,000 people

00:45:26.800 --> 00:45:28.860
in a 23-year trajectory.

00:45:29.040 --> 00:45:30.380
That's pretty remarkable.

00:45:30.800 --> 00:45:35.840
It didn't come because everybody that he talked to was only from the Banu Hashim.

00:45:36.720 --> 00:45:38.000
It just simply didn't.

00:45:38.000 --> 00:45:42.700
And the s is an important thing to read because it makes the Quran concrete.

00:45:43.440 --> 00:45:49.560
It just breaks down any justification we have to say this is why my masid

00:45:50.000 --> 00:45:54.960
turned into a place where people who have as much wealth as I do and speak

00:45:54.960 --> 00:45:59.520
the same language as I do or have all of these other characteristics in common with

00:45:59.520 --> 00:45:59.860
me

00:46:00.319 --> 00:46:06.400
run it and decide how people who are from diverse backgrounds are allowed to have access

00:46:06.400 --> 00:46:10.160
to it. That's not how you build effective community.

00:46:10.160 --> 00:46:11.440
Just go and talk to people.

00:46:11.440 --> 00:46:16.020
I've had grown men man who are in their 50s sitting in my office in tears.

00:46:16.400 --> 00:46:17.680
I'll say, "Why are you crying?"

00:46:17.680 --> 00:46:20.700
And they'll say, "Nobody's ever asked me what I think before."

00:46:22.160 --> 00:46:22.620
Why?

00:46:23.680 --> 00:46:26.299
Why would you want to pretend like you know everything?

00:46:26.480 --> 00:46:30.179
Yeah. How is that a recipe or a key to Jenna?

00:46:30.480 --> 00:46:30.940
How?

00:46:32.720 --> 00:46:38.319
rather than the approach that says I don't know everything and I'm willing to hear and

00:46:38.319 --> 00:46:39.579
listen and hold

00:46:40.800 --> 00:46:45.440
and then allow for you to know that I acknowledge that your story is still a

00:46:45.440 --> 00:46:49.280
part of this bigger thing and even if we can't do it now I didn't build

00:46:49.280 --> 00:46:54.140
a shelter in year one of our work we built a shelter in the year 2019

00:46:54.640 --> 00:46:56.539
I started working there in 2005

00:46:57.200 --> 00:46:59.040
but I knew having met

00:46:59.440 --> 00:47:04.480
one to 12 women a week who have know men in their life that have an

00:47:04.480 --> 00:47:06.380
understanding of what it means to be a man.

00:47:07.440 --> 00:47:09.879
That it wasn't just about sitting with them,

00:47:10.319 --> 00:47:15.440
but using a platform that Allah endowed me with to say, "We got to build something

00:47:15.440 --> 00:47:16.140
institutional

00:47:16.640 --> 00:47:21.260
to try to help as many break out of this beyond just a one-on-one discussion."

00:47:21.359 --> 00:47:26.480
It takes time, but if you know what a community is working towards objectively, then you're

00:47:26.480 --> 00:47:28.059
going to fulfill the objectives.

00:47:29.839 --> 00:47:30.539
Mashallah.

00:47:30.960 --> 00:47:32.480
I started taking notes in the middle.

00:47:32.480 --> 00:47:35.359
I'm like, this is too good for me to sit around and not take notes.

00:47:35.359 --> 00:47:36.860
May Allah bless you.

00:47:37.200 --> 00:47:39.140
Um, so I think um

00:47:39.920 --> 00:47:40.820
I'm going to

00:47:41.359 --> 00:47:41.699
uh

00:47:42.640 --> 00:47:44.239
lighten up the mood a little bit

00:47:44.640 --> 00:47:47.040
inshallah and then we'll get back to some questions.

00:47:47.040 --> 00:47:49.740
So these are some rapid fire questions that I have for you.

00:47:49.920 --> 00:47:51.020
So we get to know you.

00:47:51.760 --> 00:47:54.800
You've you've told a lot about you but you know we'll get to know.

00:47:54.800 --> 00:47:58.220
So these are basically faith and identity related questions inshallah.

00:47:59.440 --> 00:48:04.240
Was there a moment in your youth when you knew you were going to dedicate your

00:48:04.240 --> 00:48:05.860
life to this cause?

00:48:07.359 --> 00:48:07.699
Uh

00:48:08.160 --> 00:48:08.500
no

00:48:09.280 --> 00:48:10.640
I think I just kind of got

00:48:11.520 --> 00:48:12.560
turned into it.

00:48:12.560 --> 00:48:16.719
Alhamdulillah. Alhamdulillah. But I I couldn't tell you here's this one moment.

00:48:16.800 --> 00:48:20.659
I could tell you that I met people who were just very nice people

00:48:21.119 --> 00:48:21.999
uh when I was

00:48:22.720 --> 00:48:24.700
17 and went to my undergrad.

00:48:24.720 --> 00:48:27.340
The first Muslim I met was Indonesian.

00:48:27.440 --> 00:48:29.079
I know this is not rapid fire.

00:48:29.280 --> 00:48:32.560
Um I had never met an Indonesian Muslim in my life before.

00:48:33.200 --> 00:48:36.640
And he was also one of the kindest human beings I ever met in my life.

00:48:36.640 --> 00:48:38.100
His name is Rwan Falah.

00:48:38.400 --> 00:48:39.840
Really beautiful person.

00:48:39.920 --> 00:48:41.500
I still keep in touch with him.

00:48:42.000 --> 00:48:43.840
He was one of the first people who

00:48:44.480 --> 00:48:47.180
all I knew of him was just warmth and compassion.

00:48:47.440 --> 00:48:52.800
We literally were walking down the street and a brother gave him salams, commented on how

00:48:52.800 --> 00:48:56.880
he liked his scarf and he just took the scarf off his neck and put it

00:48:56.880 --> 00:49:01.019
on him and says, "It's for you, but not with any sense that was transactional."

00:49:01.520 --> 00:49:05.019
And I said, "I've never met somebody like this before."

00:49:05.440 --> 00:49:09.520
But he also was one who encouraged me to believe that I could have a relationship

00:49:09.520 --> 00:49:14.800
with Allah. And Allah blessed me to meet people like that who despite what were perceived

00:49:14.800 --> 00:49:21.040
inadequacies growing up going to a masid where everybody pointed out whatever reason I shouldn't be

00:49:21.040 --> 00:49:26.240
there. He was the first person who helped me to understand that that's not what this

00:49:26.240 --> 00:49:27.600
dean is rooted in.

00:49:28.000 --> 00:49:31.420
And I don't know if I had not met him where I would be right now.

00:49:31.760 --> 00:49:34.100
No, but it was just kindness and warmth

00:49:34.559 --> 00:49:37.999
that was this is what Islam should be or bring you to.

00:49:38.800 --> 00:49:42.659
What is one misconception people have about you that you're

00:49:43.680 --> 00:49:47.120
that that they have about you because you are a public religious figure?

00:49:48.160 --> 00:49:48.960
Oh, I don't know.

00:49:48.960 --> 00:49:50.920
I don't even think about things like that.

00:49:51.040 --> 00:49:53.760
Uh what is a misconception people have about me?

00:49:53.760 --> 00:49:58.240
I think when I go to travel places, a lot of times people comment who have

00:49:58.240 --> 00:50:02.640
only seen me on like a screen of some kind that they didn't think I was

00:50:02.640 --> 00:50:04.080
as short as I actually am.

00:50:04.160 --> 00:50:07.440
They said that I speak with a voice that seems like I'm a lot taller than

00:50:07.440 --> 00:50:08.720
I am. And I said, "I'm sorry.

00:50:08.720 --> 00:50:10.940
This is just how big Allah made me."

00:50:10.960 --> 00:50:13.240
Yeah. Uh what's the hardest thing

00:50:13.599 --> 00:50:17.019
hardest part of being a Muslim leader in America right now for you?

00:50:18.000 --> 00:50:21.520
Uh, you know, alhamdulillah, I'm blessed to have a really good community, but I had a

00:50:21.520 --> 00:50:25.400
teacher who when I first started working in this role, he said,

00:50:26.079 --> 00:50:26.479
uh,

00:50:27.119 --> 00:50:28.760
you're perfect for this,

00:50:29.599 --> 00:50:31.680
but get ready is going to be very lonely.

00:50:31.680 --> 00:50:33.620
And I couldn't understand what he meant.

00:50:34.000 --> 00:50:37.600
Uh, and for a long period of time, it was very lonely.

00:50:37.920 --> 00:50:39.660
My self-care was non-existent.

00:50:39.839 --> 00:50:41.800
I was getting sick all of the time.

00:50:42.000 --> 00:50:46.100
I was building a lot of just inward angst.

00:50:46.880 --> 00:50:51.680
And Allah has blessed me with the community that I serve that even as large as

00:50:51.680 --> 00:50:54.539
it is, they check in on their leadership.

00:50:55.040 --> 00:50:59.839
But I had to be willing to actually acknowledge that it was okay for me to

00:50:59.839 --> 00:51:01.380
let people be there for me

00:51:01.839 --> 00:51:06.240
rather than having to tell myself I just have to be there for everyone else all

00:51:06.240 --> 00:51:07.400
of the time.

00:51:07.920 --> 00:51:11.639
And so I think that was something that was a little bit hard.

00:51:11.839 --> 00:51:14.860
And I see it with a lot of Muslim leaders right now.

00:51:14.880 --> 00:51:19.839
You don't fundamentally know what it's like when somebody reaches out to you with a death

00:51:19.839 --> 00:51:20.479
threat.

00:51:20.960 --> 00:51:22.280
Like, you just don't know.

00:51:22.880 --> 00:51:26.419
You don't know what it's like when somebody comments on

00:51:26.960 --> 00:51:27.920
every single thing.

00:51:27.920 --> 00:51:29.760
I'm part of a community that does not do this.

00:51:29.760 --> 00:51:30.460
Alhamdulillah.

00:51:30.960 --> 00:51:33.200
But I have kids the way you all have kids.

00:51:33.200 --> 00:51:35.500
I'm married the way some of you are married.

00:51:35.520 --> 00:51:36.559
I have a family.

00:51:36.559 --> 00:51:40.640
I'm not the only one who my name is attached to, but there are others.

00:51:40.720 --> 00:51:43.599
And I don't want my kids to feel like they have to live in my shadow

00:51:43.599 --> 00:51:45.220
or anything to that extent

00:51:45.599 --> 00:51:49.040
and to understand the recognition of what we've been blessed with.

00:51:49.040 --> 00:51:52.800
I see a lot of my peers and friends who they don't have the same thing.

00:51:52.800 --> 00:51:54.539
People treat them like garbage.

00:51:55.280 --> 00:52:00.059
You don't want to be people who treat your like community leaders in that way.

00:52:00.240 --> 00:52:01.520
And you evoke support.

00:52:01.520 --> 00:52:02.940
You exert compassion.

00:52:03.119 --> 00:52:07.660
You don't treat people in a way that dishonors the rights that they have over you.

00:52:08.000 --> 00:52:12.620
And so that loneliness variable is important to be able to understand.

00:52:12.640 --> 00:52:17.259
There's still stuff I go through today that people just might not relate experientially.

00:52:18.000 --> 00:52:19.699
But I have to be willing to

00:52:20.160 --> 00:52:20.860
understand

00:52:21.359 --> 00:52:21.819
that

00:52:22.319 --> 00:52:24.160
love is a very important variable.

00:52:24.160 --> 00:52:25.740
And I'll share this anecdote.

00:52:25.760 --> 00:52:28.559
I had a student of mine who's in California.

00:52:28.559 --> 00:52:30.040
His name is Afras Khan.

00:52:30.319 --> 00:52:35.160
He graduated from our undergrad, did his MBA, does a lot of really good work.

00:52:35.440 --> 00:52:40.160
uh he was doing a semester abroad and we were on a Zoom with some of

00:52:40.160 --> 00:52:41.660
his other classmates

00:52:42.240 --> 00:52:44.419
and I hadn't seen him in months

00:52:45.280 --> 00:52:46.940
and when we got on the Zoom

00:52:47.440 --> 00:52:48.340
he got on

00:52:48.960 --> 00:52:49.360
and

00:52:50.160 --> 00:52:50.500
he

00:52:51.440 --> 00:52:54.800
you know at first it was pretty comical because he hadn't cut his hair in a

00:52:54.800 --> 00:52:58.480
long time and his beard was very he looked like he hadn't showered in a while

00:52:58.480 --> 00:52:59.699
and I was like what's going on

00:53:00.240 --> 00:53:04.380
and then when he said salams to me he just started crying so much

00:53:04.960 --> 00:53:08.760
Oh, and people didn't know what to do on the Zoom.

00:53:09.200 --> 00:53:13.599
And when I turned off the Zoom, I was about 30 years old when this happened,

00:53:13.599 --> 00:53:14.539
12 years ago.

00:53:15.760 --> 00:53:19.879
And I turned it off and I reflected and I said, "Man,

00:53:20.800 --> 00:53:23.060
this kid loves me so much."

00:53:24.720 --> 00:53:29.680
And I had never really experienced what it means to love someone for the sake of

00:53:29.680 --> 00:53:33.400
Allah. And acknowledging that that was a thing that I could name.

00:53:34.240 --> 00:53:38.479
And when I was open to embracing the fact that he loves me,

00:53:39.040 --> 00:53:44.140
it created an opening for me to say these other people actually love me too.

00:53:45.119 --> 00:53:47.840
And then that opened a door in which I could say

00:53:48.319 --> 00:53:49.639
I love them also.

00:53:51.040 --> 00:53:53.699
And there's so much in our tradition, right?

00:54:08.160 --> 00:54:09.780
We can talk about it again.

00:54:09.920 --> 00:54:11.480
Love, love, love.

00:54:11.520 --> 00:54:11.980
Why?

00:54:13.119 --> 00:54:17.019
Because when you are bold enough to love someone,

00:54:17.680 --> 00:54:21.760
it'll give you a courage unlike anything you imagine.

00:54:21.760 --> 00:54:22.160
Ya.

00:54:23.280 --> 00:54:28.299
And when you are willing to acknowledge the love that someone has for you,

00:54:28.720 --> 00:54:32.359
it will give you a strength unlike anything also.

00:54:32.800 --> 00:54:38.160
And what Shayan sometimes does is have us chase after the things that we already possess.

00:54:38.160 --> 00:54:38.620
Adam

00:54:39.200 --> 00:54:40.719
is told not to eat from the

00:54:43.040 --> 00:54:46.000
heats it, but what's he going to get from the tree of eternality?

00:54:46.000 --> 00:54:47.139
He's already in Jenna.

00:54:47.839 --> 00:54:52.160
it's not going to do anything for him in the way that he already possesses what

00:54:52.160 --> 00:54:53.040
it offers.

00:54:54.720 --> 00:54:55.900
And that loneliness

00:54:56.319 --> 00:54:58.179
is really, really heavy.

00:54:58.800 --> 00:55:03.119
And I can tell you being on the other side of it, when people say to

00:55:03.119 --> 00:55:05.699
me, "How do you get done what you get done?"

00:55:06.160 --> 00:55:09.160
I tell my community all the time that I love them.

00:55:09.440 --> 00:55:12.640
As much as I tell my children every day that I love them, I tell my

00:55:12.640 --> 00:55:14.460
wife every day that I love her,

00:55:15.520 --> 00:55:21.900
it is something that I can do and they experience with conviction because I also love

00:55:21.920 --> 00:55:22.560
myself.

00:55:23.119 --> 00:55:27.259
And it's hard to fully love outwardly when you don't like yourself.

00:55:27.839 --> 00:55:33.519
You cannot fundamentally like yourself if you need everyone around you to be just like you

00:55:33.520 --> 00:55:35.340
in order to feel comfortable.

00:55:36.319 --> 00:55:41.040
and getting to a place where you can acknowledge even if there's that one person who's

00:55:41.040 --> 00:55:41.980
there with you,

00:55:42.400 --> 00:55:44.700
it can then ignite a

00:55:45.599 --> 00:55:50.479
fire that allows for you to say, "I don't have to confine myself to this dunya."

00:55:50.480 --> 00:55:51.319
Do you know?

00:55:51.839 --> 00:55:56.620
But that loneliness is what a lot of Muslim leaders are experiencing right now.

00:55:56.880 --> 00:56:01.280
And you don't want to be a community that contributes to that when you are building

00:56:01.280 --> 00:56:05.920
what you're building here or as she said that there's a lot of other I don't

00:56:05.920 --> 00:56:07.599
know how any masid runs here.

00:56:07.599 --> 00:56:08.680
Do you know what I mean?

00:56:09.040 --> 00:56:14.280
But if you've been entrusted to be a caretaker of Allah's house remember it's Allah's house

00:56:14.319 --> 00:56:16.400
and every guest has rights.

00:56:16.480 --> 00:56:16.940
Yaam

00:56:19.040 --> 00:56:21.180
is a big part of our tradition

00:56:22.000 --> 00:56:26.079
and that means the person who you brought in to lead the community is still a

00:56:26.079 --> 00:56:27.460
guest in Allah's house.

00:56:27.760 --> 00:56:29.460
You treat them like a guest.

00:56:29.520 --> 00:56:31.200
You don't treat them like a servant.

00:56:31.200 --> 00:56:33.139
They are Allah's abd, not yours.

00:56:33.599 --> 00:56:34.380
Do you know

00:56:38.480 --> 00:56:42.860
they were supposed to be light rapid fires, but alhamdulillah the on to something.

00:56:42.880 --> 00:56:44.960
So let's go to some easy questions.

00:56:44.960 --> 00:56:46.080
Tea or coffee?

00:56:46.799 --> 00:56:49.120
I don't really drink either tea or coffee.

00:56:50.480 --> 00:56:53.040
It's not. I don't think this rap fire is going anywhere.

00:56:53.040 --> 00:56:53.560
Yeah.

00:56:54.079 --> 00:56:56.820
Okay, cool. Uh, biryani or mandi?

00:56:58.240 --> 00:56:59.920
Oh, I like both.

00:56:59.920 --> 00:57:01.960
I'm not I'm not going to be good at this.

00:57:02.240 --> 00:57:06.000
I I like I You have such diplomatic New Yorker answers.

00:57:06.000 --> 00:57:07.280
I just I like everything.

00:57:07.280 --> 00:57:08.620
I don't know what to tell you.

00:57:08.960 --> 00:57:09.920
I told him, "What do you want?"

00:57:09.920 --> 00:57:12.079
Anything. Like, I'm a 5-year-old.

00:57:12.079 --> 00:57:12.720
Take me anywhere.

00:57:12.720 --> 00:57:13.760
I I'll eat anything.

00:57:13.760 --> 00:57:14.520
It's true.

00:57:14.880 --> 00:57:17.999
Okay. If you had an extra hour in your day, what would you do?

00:57:19.680 --> 00:57:20.080
Uh,

00:57:20.640 --> 00:57:24.240
I would hang out with my kids more, but we already do that a lot.

00:57:24.240 --> 00:57:25.160
I don't know.

00:57:25.200 --> 00:57:28.240
I mean, I've I've brought a lot of structure to my routine.

00:57:28.240 --> 00:57:30.240
Took me a while to put come up with these questions.

00:57:30.240 --> 00:57:31.600
I'm sorry. Uh,

00:57:32.319 --> 00:57:35.359
I mean, I think I would do a little bit more of the things I do

00:57:35.359 --> 00:57:35.819
now.

00:57:36.400 --> 00:57:36.800
Um,

00:57:37.680 --> 00:57:38.080
but

00:57:39.119 --> 00:57:41.460
you know, I said this at Juma, I think, today.

00:57:42.240 --> 00:57:44.959
I don't remember because I did a talk right after online.

00:57:45.440 --> 00:57:48.880
I meet a lot of people who are going to pass away from the world when

00:57:48.880 --> 00:57:51.200
they're in their last moments in the work that I do.

00:57:51.200 --> 00:57:56.419
When you serve a community that's 20,000 people, every day somebody you know is dying.

00:57:58.000 --> 00:58:02.700
And when I sit with people at the end of their life, I've never met somebody

00:58:02.880 --> 00:58:06.359
who says to me, "I wish I had five more minutes to go to work.

00:58:07.200 --> 00:58:10.960
I wish I had five more minutes to gossip or be racist or to be mean

00:58:10.960 --> 00:58:12.340
to people at the masid.

00:58:12.400 --> 00:58:16.000
Yeah. What they say is, I wish I had more time to just spend with my

00:58:16.000 --> 00:58:16.680
loved ones,

00:58:17.520 --> 00:58:20.160
to be a part of things that I wish I hadn't let go of.

00:58:20.160 --> 00:58:22.000
You don't want to be afraid of failure.

00:58:22.000 --> 00:58:24.160
That's a stupid thing to be afraid of.

00:58:24.880 --> 00:58:27.680
This is not a religion that teaches us to pursue perfection.

00:58:27.680 --> 00:58:29.920
What you do want to be afraid of is regret.

00:58:31.040 --> 00:58:34.960
A regret that's unhealthy that says, "Why did I do it in the way that I

00:58:34.960 --> 00:58:35.579
did it?"

00:58:36.000 --> 00:58:40.059
So, if you were to ask me what my work life balance looks like right now,

00:58:40.160 --> 00:58:44.940
well, I spent a lot of time identifying and defining the way we talked about community.

00:58:45.119 --> 00:58:48.299
What does life mean in a work life balance?

00:58:48.559 --> 00:58:53.120
And I don't have to give it up in ways that sometimes people assume you do.

00:58:53.359 --> 00:58:58.000
There's a lot more time in the day that we can structure things and allowing for

00:58:58.000 --> 00:59:03.200
us. If I had another hour to my day, I think I would just create more

00:59:03.200 --> 00:59:06.400
continuity to some of what it is that's going on.

00:59:06.400 --> 00:59:08.079
But I had to learn the hard way.

00:59:08.079 --> 00:59:09.040
When I was 29,

00:59:09.680 --> 00:59:10.779
I got shingles.

00:59:10.880 --> 00:59:13.200
I don't know if anybody here has ever had shingles before.

00:59:13.200 --> 00:59:14.299
You've had shingles.

00:59:14.400 --> 00:59:15.499
Was it enjoyable?

00:59:16.000 --> 00:59:17.200
No. It's terrible.

00:59:17.200 --> 00:59:20.960
Right. Yeah. And I'm not commenting on your age.

00:59:20.960 --> 00:59:22.599
She forgive me for that.

00:59:22.799 --> 00:59:23.480
You know,

00:59:24.240 --> 00:59:26.299
yeah. Mashallah. Allah preserve you.

00:59:26.960 --> 00:59:29.019
20 year olds don't get shingles.

00:59:29.839 --> 00:59:34.240
You get shingles when you're at such a high level of stress and low point of

00:59:34.240 --> 00:59:41.259
immunity. And if you ever had chickenpox, the virus reactivates in your nervous system as blisters

00:59:41.280 --> 00:59:42.500
on a nerve track.

00:59:42.559 --> 00:59:46.160
I woke up one morning and from the middle of my torso to my back in

00:59:46.160 --> 00:59:48.419
my 20s, I had this thing.

00:59:48.480 --> 00:59:49.520
I went to the doctor.

00:59:49.520 --> 00:59:50.799
They said, "Get ready.

00:59:50.799 --> 00:59:52.460
It's going to hurt a lot.

00:59:52.640 --> 00:59:57.040
It's going to feel like somebody is stabbing you again and again while they rub a

00:59:57.040 --> 00:59:58.520
hot iron on your body.

00:59:58.880 --> 01:00:02.880
I'd spent most of my 20s in a place where I was coasting off of wellness

01:00:02.880 --> 01:00:03.860
for my teens.

01:00:03.920 --> 01:00:07.180
In my senior year of high school, I got multiple concussions.

01:00:07.280 --> 01:00:09.359
I was supposed to play sports in college.

01:00:09.359 --> 01:00:13.999
To give you an idea, when I was 15 years old, I could bench press 315

01:00:14.640 --> 01:00:16.520
pounds. I ran a 4540,

01:00:17.359 --> 01:00:20.380
was very athletic, working out three times a day.

01:00:20.559 --> 01:00:25.760
And when you go through that type of rigorous agenda, you can coast off of it

01:00:25.760 --> 01:00:26.480
for a while.

01:00:26.480 --> 01:00:30.160
When I started working in this role when I was 22,

01:00:30.799 --> 01:00:34.860
a lot of my sense of wellness and structure just diminished.

01:00:35.200 --> 01:00:39.259
I wasn't spending time with people in any meaningful way.

01:00:39.359 --> 01:00:42.760
I was always starting my day with anxiety in a rush.

01:00:42.799 --> 01:00:44.059
My weight fluctuated.

01:00:44.160 --> 01:00:46.020
I get very heavy, very light.

01:00:46.079 --> 01:00:48.220
And then this shingles thing happened.

01:00:48.559 --> 01:00:54.079
After about seven years of not paying attention to the signs that my mind, my body,

01:00:54.079 --> 01:00:55.680
my heart were showing to me.

01:00:56.319 --> 01:01:02.160
I had to quarantine for a while because until the blisters pop and the liquid releases,

01:01:02.160 --> 01:01:03.180
which is contagious,

01:01:03.599 --> 01:01:04.880
you got to be in quarantine.

01:01:04.880 --> 01:01:08.480
So, I went to my parents house where I grew up and I stayed in the

01:01:08.480 --> 01:01:09.920
bedroom that I grew up in.

01:01:10.000 --> 01:01:15.599
And one night I was checking my email and I got an email from a student

01:01:15.599 --> 01:01:17.999
of mine who said earlier that day

01:01:18.400 --> 01:01:25.220
her Muslim boyfriend grabbed her by the straps of her book bag in the hallway of

01:01:25.280 --> 01:01:28.940
their classroom building and threw her against some lockers.

01:01:29.200 --> 01:01:32.959
And she said had a security guard not intervened, she wouldn't know what to do.

01:01:33.040 --> 01:01:35.480
And she was reaching out to me now for help.

01:01:35.680 --> 01:01:39.839
and I closed the laptop and I went downstairs to where my mother was sitting watching

01:01:39.839 --> 01:01:43.180
